# Clean Code — General Principles

## PRCP-01 — Keep it simple

Simpler is always better. Reduce complexity as much as possible. If two solutions solve the same problem, choose the one with fewer moving parts.

## PRCP-02 — Boy Scout Rule

Leave the code cleaner than you found it. Fix small messes — rename a confusing variable, extract a long block, remove dead code — even if you didn't introduce them.

## PRCP-03 — Find the root cause

Do not patch symptoms. When a bug or design problem appears, trace it to the underlying cause before writing a fix.

## PRCP-04 — Follow standard conventions

Honour the conventions of the team and the language. Consistency within a codebase matters more than personal preference.

## PRCP-05 — Prefer polymorphism over conditionals

Replace if/else or switch/case chains that branch on type or kind with polymorphism. Adding a new case then requires a new class, not a modified branch.

```
// bad
function area(shape)
  if shape.type == "circle"
    return PI * shape.radius ^ 2
  else if shape.type == "rect"
    return shape.width * shape.height
  end if
end function

// good
function area(shape)
  return shape.area()
end function
```

## PRCP-06 — Use dependency injection

Pass dependencies into a class rather than constructing them inside it. Hard-coded instantiation couples the class to a concrete implementation.

```
// bad
class ReportService
  db = new PostgresDatabase()
end class

// good
class ReportService
  constructor(db)
    this.db = db
  end constructor
end class
```

## PRCP-07 — Law of Demeter

A method should call only methods on: itself, its own fields, its arguments, and objects it directly creates. Do not reach through intermediate objects.

```
// bad — reaching through address then city
customer.getAddress().getCity().getPostCode()

// good — ask the object you know
customer.getPostCode()
```

## PRCP-08 — Avoid negative conditionals

Write positive conditional expressions. Negation adds cognitive overhead and is easy to misread.

```
// bad
if not user.isNotActive()

// good
if user.isActive()
```

## PRCP-09 — Encapsulate boundary conditions

Boundary calculations are error-prone. Name them once rather than scattering the raw expression.

```
// bad
if offset + pageSize < results.length
  showNextPage()
end if

// good
hasNextPage = offset + pageSize < results.length
if hasNextPage
  showNextPage()
end if
```

## PRCP-10 — Avoid logical dependency between methods

A method must not silently rely on another method having been called first to set state. Each method should be correct given only its own arguments and invariants.

## PRCP-11 — Prefer dedicated value objects over primitives

Use small, named types for domain concepts rather than passing bare primitives. A `Money` type is harder to misuse than a raw `float`.
